<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- CFBundleDevelopmentRegion stays "it": that's the source language of
	     Localizable.xcstrings (QAudionApp/Localizable.xcstrings) and the
	     language every string was originally authored/reviewed in.
	     Separately, set the "Italian" primary language in App Store
	     Connect's app metadata, that's a dashboard setting, not derived
	     from this key. -->
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>it</string>
	<!-- W-L10N-BATCH1 (2026-09-08) — real supported-language set, so App
	     Store Connect and iOS itself see it (affects the Settings.app
	     per-app language picker and App Store listing language matching).
	     Order: dev region first, then the rest alphabetical by code —
	     matches no particular priority, iOS doesn't treat array order as
	     ranking. Batch 2 (ru/ar/zh-Hans/ja/nl/tr/pl/ko) is deliberately
	     NOT listed yet — see the l10n plan doc, this is Batch 1 only. -->
	<key>CFBundleLocalizations</key>
	<array>
		<string>it</string>
		<string>en</string>
		<string>es</string>
		<string>fr</string>
		<string>de</string>
		<string>pt-BR</string>
	</array>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Q-Audion</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_PACKAGE_TYPE)</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>1.0.1155</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>1</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Q-Audion uses your microphone for end-to-end encrypted voice and video calls, for voice messages in chats, and for voice authentication.</string>
	<key>NFCReaderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Q-Audion reads NFC tags to securely import encryption keys from Android devices.</string>
	<!-- W-NFCIOS  -  the AID of the Android HCE service we tap against
	     (NfcConstants.AID_HEX, apduservice.xml). Without this array CoreNFC
	     never sends SELECT for our application, so it never surfaces an
	     NFCISO7816Tag: the reader session finds nothing and dies on its own
	     timeout. Confirmed live 2026-07-22 on an iPhone-to-S26 tap  -  the
	     Android side logged the field arriving and SELECTs going to off-host
	     (secure element) AIDs only, with zero HostEmulation dispatch to
	     com.bcrypto.qaudion.nfc.NfcApduService.

	     It belongs HERE and not in QAudion.entitlements, even though Apple
	     files its documentation page under bundleresources/entitlements. Put
	     in the entitlements file it fails the archive outright:
	       "Entitlement com.apple.developer.nfc.readersession.iso7816.select-identifiers
	        not found and could not be included in profile."
	     The sibling `readersession.formats` key IS a real entitlement and
	     stays there; only the AID list is read from Info.plist.

	     This is also NOT the restricted card-emulation entitlement. That one
	     is com.apple.developer.nfc.hce (the CardSession API, which Apple
	     documents for payments / car keys / transit / badges / hotel keys /
	     loyalty / event tickets in the EEA and requires applying for), and it
	     is why an iPhone cannot be the card  -  which bounds iPhone-to-iPhone
	     tapping, not this. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.nfc.readersession.iso7816.select-identifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>F0BCF1073A5100</string>
	</array>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Q-Audion uses the camera for end-to-end encrypted video calls, to take photos you send in chats or set as your avatar, and to scan QR codes when pairing contacts or importing keys.</string>
	<key>NSContactsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Q-Audion can use your contacts so you can import them into your encrypted address book, and to automatically show the name and photo of a phone contact when they call you or you call them. To find contacts already on Q-Audion, phone numbers are hashed on your device before matching; names and photos never leave your phone.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Q-Audion saves chat images you choose to keep into your Photos library.</string>
	<key>NSFaceIDUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Q-Audion uses Face ID to unlock the app when the app lock feature is enabled.</string>
	<!-- NSBluetoothPeripheralUsageDescription (deprecated since iOS 13) is
	     intentionally absent: the Always key covers every supported iOS. -->
	<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Q-Audion uses Bluetooth to pair with Q-Audion earbuds for hardware-backed key custody and, only when you turn on the mesh antenna in a chat, to relay encrypted messages to nearby Q-Audion devices when no network is available.</string>
	<!-- WebRTC gathers host ICE candidates, so a call between two devices on
	     the same Wi-Fi can connect directly; iOS 14+ shows the local-network
	     prompt for that and requires this string to be present. -->
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Q-Audion connects directly to other Q-Audion devices on your local network for lower-latency encrypted calls.</string>
	<!-- CarPlay/Siri state-of-the-art plan S1 — required by Apple whenever
	     the app calls INPreferences.requestSiriAuthorization (AppState.
	     initialize()); the system shows this exact string in the
	     authorization alert. Apple's own doc: "This key is a requirement" —
	     missing it is a hard App Review blocker, not just a style nit. -->
	<key>NSSiriUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Q-Audion shares your contact names with Siri so you can start an encrypted call by saying "Call [name] on Q-Audion".</string>
	<!-- CarPlay/Siri state-of-the-art plan S1 (docs/superpowers/plans/
	     2026-09-06-carplay-state-of-the-art.md) — declares that this app can
	     continue an NSUserActivity of type "INStartCallIntent". SiriKit hands
	     this activity to `.onContinueUserActivity` in QAudionApp.swift after
	     the QAudionIntents extension (IntentHandler.swift) resolves a "Hey
	     Siri, chiama X su Q-Audion" request. The string MUST be the literal
	     Intents class name, not a custom identifier — this is Apple's own
	     convention for every INStartCallIntent-based VoIP app. -->
	<key>NSUserActivityTypes</key>
	<array>
		<string>INStartCallIntent</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<false/>
	</dict>
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict/>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>voip</string>
		<string>audio</string>
		<string>fetch</string>
	</array>
	<key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>com.bcrypto.qaudion.ws-keepalive</string>
	</array>
	<!-- LiveKit screen-share (ReplayKit broadcast upload extension): override
	     BroadcastBundleInfo's bundle-id-derived defaults because this app's
	     bundle id (com.qaudion.app) doesn't share the "com.bcrypto.qaudion.*"
	     prefix this project's App Groups use. Both keys are read via
	     Bundle.main.infoDictionary, see client-sdk-swift
	     Sources/LiveKit/Broadcast/BroadcastBundleInfo.swift.
	     RTCAppGroupIdentifier is ALSO set (identical value) in
	     QAudionBroadcastExtension/Info.plist: each process (main app vs.
	     extension) reads its OWN Bundle.main, so the override must be present
	     on BOTH sides or the IPC socket path diverges between them. -->
	<key>RTCAppGroupIdentifier</key>
	<string>group.com.bcrypto.qaudion.broadcast</string>
	<key>RTCScreenSharingExtension</key>
	<string>com.qaudion.app.broadcast</string>
	<!--
	  Export Compliance - required by Apple to skip the manual
	  "Provide Export Compliance Information" prompt on every upload.
	  Position (2026-09-12, App Store readiness audit, pending counsel
	  confirmation before the store submission is sent): the app uses
	  only standard, published algorithms - AES-256-GCM, ChaCha20-
	  Poly1305 (WireGuard), X25519, Ed25519, SHA-2/HKDF, and ML-KEM-1024
	  (NIST FIPS 203) via liboqs - implemented by open-source libraries,
	  and encryption is used for the app's own communications, not sold
	  as an encryption product. That is the "uses standard encryption
	  algorithms" self-classification Apple lists as not requiring an
	  ERN/CCATS code; the annual BIS self-classification report still
	  applies to the account holder. If counsel disagrees, set this to
	  true and add ITSEncryptionExportComplianceCode with the code App
	  Store Connect issues. France: ANSSI declaration before enabling
	  France in availability.
	  NOTE: this file is intentionally pure ASCII. A non-ASCII em-dash
	  here was repeatedly mojibaked by the release script's encoding
	  handling and ballooned Info.plist to 66 MB by v1.0.471 (W472).
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
